#
# The second line after a `$( … )` body's refusal — #3331.
#
# The sentence names the closer since #3296. This shell then writes a second
# message, and the text in it is the script's rather than the body's: here
# bash quotes the **whole script line** the refusal was found on — not the
# body, and not the text from the substitution on. The body is read at expansion time, so that text has to be handed
# to the runner by whatever read the script, which is what the file grades.
#
# The body runs onto a second line and something else shares both of its
# lines, which is what separates the three readings: the script's line, the
# line the command began on, and the body. The failure's line is also the
# one the first message names, where it used to name the line the command
# began on.
#
# A copy of the shape in zsh/substitution-echo.tests rather than a shared file, because the two
# answers differ and a <dialect>/ file is one only that column writes.
#
# One failing case, and it is last: the failure ends the script.

printf 'the controls first\n'

# A body that parses, which is what says the body is read at all.
v=$(echo hi); printf 'an ordinary body -> [%s]\n' "$v"

printf 'the case\n'
q=1; v=$(echo hi
 for); z=2
printf 'never reached\n'
